- Acoustic deterrent devices (ADDs) emit underwater sound pulses to keep marine mammals away from fishing nets, reducing bycatch.
- A 2022 study in the Firth of Clyde found that ADDs reduced harbor porpoise bycatch by 83% over a 14-month trial.
- Critics note that ADDs may displace porpoises from important feeding grounds, potentially causing long-term habitat exclusion.
- Alternative bycatch reduction methods, such as modified net designs, have shown only a 29% reduction in porpoise bycatch in comparable trials.
- The International Whaling Commission has called for more research into the long-term behavioral effects of ADDs on cetacean populations.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Acoustic deterrent devices reduced harbor porpoise bycatch by 83% in a Firth of Clyde trial, and the International Whaling Commission has called for more research into their long-term behavioral effects on cetacean populations."},{"key":"B","text":"Although critics note that acoustic deterrent devices may displace porpoises from feeding grounds, the devices reduced bycatch by 83% — far exceeding the 29% reduction achieved by modified net designs — making them a compelling candidate for further research investment."},{"key":"C","text":"Modified net designs have achieved only a 29% reduction in porpoise bycatch, while acoustic deterrent devices achieved an 83% reduction in a 2022 Firth of Clyde study lasting 14 months."},{"key":"D","text":"Because acoustic deterrent devices may cause long-term habitat exclusion for porpoises, the International Whaling Commission has recommended further study of their behavioral effects on cetacean populations."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'The choice beginning "Although critics note that acoustic deterrent devices may displace porpoises" most effectively argues for further investment despite limitations: it acknowledges the criticism (habitat displacement), counters it with the strong efficacy data (83% bycatch reduction), and contrasts that result with the weaker alternative (29% from modified nets), building a case that the devices'' demonstrated superiority justifies continued funding. The choice mentioning the 83% reduction and the IWC call for research presents both facts but does not structure them as an argument for investment despite drawbacks — it merely lists findings. The choice comparing the 29% and 83% figures provides a data comparison without acknowledging the devices'' limitations, so it does not argue "despite" anything. The choice emphasizing habitat exclusion and the IWC recommendation foregrounds the concerns rather than making a case for investment, effectively arguing against the method rather than for it.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-19"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-19","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);



Yet Stevens''s devotion to Lord Darlington — a figure whose interwar diplomacy facilitated appeasement — is not merely personal loyalty; it is ideological submission. His refusal to interrogate Darlington''s politics mirrors the broader English servant class''s complicity in maintaining hierarchies they neither questioned nor fully understood. In this light, Stevens''s unreliability is less a private pathology than a symptom of systemic deference.

Recent criticism has attempted to reconcile these perspectives by arguing that Ishiguro deliberately fuses the psychological and political registers, making Stevens''s internal evasions inseparable from the social structures that produced them.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"It presents a counterargument to the interpretation described in the first paragraph by reframing the narrator's unreliability as a product of social and political forces."},{"key":"B","text":"It provides biographical details about Kazuo Ishiguro that explain why he chose to write from an unreliable narrator's perspective."},{"key":"C","text":"It concedes that the psychological reading outlined in the first paragraph is largely correct but identifies a minor limitation."},{"key":"D","text":"It introduces a new novel by a different author to illustrate a broader trend in postwar British fiction."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'The first paragraph describes the psychological reading of Stevens''s unreliability. The second paragraph introduces the political interpretation by connecting Stevens''s deference to Lord Darlington''s appeasement politics and the servant class''s systemic complicity, explicitly reframing his unreliability as a "symptom of systemic deference" rather than a private condition. This functions as a counterargument to the purely psychological reading. The claim that the second paragraph provides biographical details about Ishiguro is wrong because no information about the author''s life appears anywhere in the passage. The claim that it concedes the psychological reading is largely correct mischaracterizes the second paragraph''s stance: rather than conceding and noting a minor flaw, it offers a fundamentally different interpretive framework that challenges the psychological reading''s premise. The claim that a different author''s novel is introduced is wrong because the entire passage discusses only Ishiguro''s The Remains of the Day.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 105, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-19"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-19","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);



- Peregrine falcons historically nested on remote cliff faces far from human habitation.
- Since the 1990s, peregrine falcons have increasingly nested on the ledges and rooftops of skyscrapers in major cities.
- A 2023 survey by the Urban Raptor Collective found 47 active peregrine nesting sites across 31 North American cities.
- Urban peregrines have a higher fledgling survival rate (78%) than their rural counterparts (61%).
- Researchers attribute the urban survival advantage to the abundance of pigeons and starlings as prey and to reduced predation from great horned owls.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"A 2023 survey by the Urban Raptor Collective found 47 active peregrine nesting sites across 31 North American cities, and researchers have studied the factors contributing to their urban success."},{"key":"B","text":"Although peregrine falcons historically nested on remote cliff faces, a 2023 survey found that urban peregrines now achieve a 78% fledgling survival rate, surpassing the 61% rate of their rural counterparts."},{"key":"C","text":"The abundance of pigeons and starlings as prey, combined with reduced predation from great horned owls, explains why peregrine falcons have increasingly nested on skyscrapers since the 1990s."},{"key":"D","text":"Since the 1990s, peregrine falcons have nested on skyscraper ledges and rooftops, with 47 active nesting sites documented across 31 North American cities by 2023."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'The choice beginning "Although peregrine falcons historically nested on remote cliff faces" most effectively emphasizes the surprising nature of the finding by juxtaposing the birds'' traditional habitat with the counterintuitive statistic that urban falcons outperform rural ones in fledgling survival. This contrast highlights how unexpected the result is. The choice citing the 47 nesting sites across 31 cities presents survey data without framing it as surprising. The choice explaining prey abundance and reduced owl predation focuses on causal mechanisms rather than on the unexpectedness of the finding. The choice describing the nesting trend since the 1990s reports a historical shift but does not draw attention to the surprising survival-rate comparison.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 75, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-19"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-19","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);












Text 2: Archaeologist Samuel Keita contends that the grain storage facilities Petrov studied are better explained by horizontal diffusion — the gradual adoption of successful innovations through trade networks and inter-community contact. Keita points to slight but consistent regional variations in storage chamber dimensions, arguing that these variations are incompatible with centralized planning but entirely consistent with independent communities adapting a shared model to local conditions.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"By maintaining that the regional variations Keita identifies are minor deviations from an imposed template rather than evidence of independent adaptation"},{"key":"B","text":"By acknowledging that horizontal diffusion explains the architectural similarities and withdrawing the claim about centralized planning"},{"key":"C","text":"By arguing that grain storage facilities appeared much earlier than 7500 BCE, undermining Keita's chronological framework"},{"key":"D","text":"By proposing that trade networks could not have existed in Mesopotamia during the period in question"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'Petrov''s argument rests on the uniform architectural design of storage facilities as evidence of centralized planning. Keita challenges this by noting regional variations that suggest independent adaptation. Petrov would most logically respond by reinterpreting those variations as minor deviations within an imposed standardized template, thereby preserving her central claim that uniformity implies top-down coordination. The suggestion that Petrov would concede to horizontal diffusion contradicts her stated position and would require abandoning her core thesis without any new evidence presented. The suggestion about an earlier date is wrong because both texts agree on the approximate 7500 BCE timeframe, and Petrov''s argument does not depend on disputing Keita''s chronology. The suggestion that trade networks could not have existed is wrong because Petrov''s argument does not deny the existence of trade networks — she argues that uniformity requires something beyond trade, namely centralized authority.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"evidence_mismatch"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 105, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-19"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-19","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);




Text 2: Sociolinguist Ravi Chandrasekaran maintains that the dominance of English in academic publishing has created a two-tier system in which researchers who publish in other languages receive substantially fewer citations and less institutional recognition. He emphasizes that this dynamic does not simply inconvenience individual scholars but systematically devalues entire intellectual traditions conducted in non-English languages.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"By arguing that the economic benefits Marques identifies come at the cost of marginalizing scholarship produced in other languages"},{"key":"B","text":"By agreeing that English fluency provides economic advantages and proposing additional benefits Marques did not mention"},{"key":"C","text":"By questioning whether English has actually spread as widely as Marques claims"},{"key":"D","text":"By suggesting that diplomatic forums should require multilingual participation rather than relying on a single language"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'Text 2 focuses on the harm English dominance causes in academic publishing — reduced citations and institutional recognition for non-English scholarship. This directly challenges Text 1''s claim that economic advantages outweigh cultural costs, because Chandrasekaran identifies a concrete systemic cost that Marques''s framing overlooks: the devaluation of entire intellectual traditions. The suggestion that Chandrasekaran would agree and add further benefits contradicts his critical stance toward English dominance. The suggestion that he would question the extent of English''s global spread is unsupported because Text 2 takes the dominance of English as a given fact rather than disputing it. The suggestion about multilingual diplomatic forums introduces a policy proposal that appears nowhere in Text 2, which focuses exclusively on academic publishing.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"evidence_mismatch"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 60, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-19"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-19","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);

- Mariam al-Asturlabi was a 10th-century instrument maker based in Aleppo, Syria.
- She designed and constructed astrolabes, devices used to map the positions of stars and planets.
- Her father, al-Ijli al-Asturlabi, was also a renowned astrolabe craftsman.
- The ruler Sayf al-Dawla employed her to build astrolabes for his court.
- Astrolabes were essential navigational and astronomical tools in the medieval Islamic world.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Mariam al-Asturlabi, a 10th-century instrument maker in Aleppo, designed astrolabes that were essential navigational and astronomical tools in the medieval Islamic world."},{"key":"B","text":"Mariam al-Asturlabi's father, al-Ijli al-Asturlabi, was also a well-known astrolabe craftsman, and both worked in Aleppo."},{"key":"C","text":"Astrolabes, which mapped the positions of stars and planets, were essential navigational tools in the medieval Islamic world."},{"key":"D","text":"The ruler Sayf al-Dawla employed Mariam al-Asturlabi to build astrolabes for his court in the 10th century."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'The choice beginning "Mariam al-Asturlabi, a 10th-century instrument maker in Aleppo" most effectively introduces both who she was and why her contribution mattered by linking her design work to the broader importance of astrolabes. The choice focusing on her father shifts attention away from Mariam''s own significance. The choice describing astrolabes in general omits Mariam entirely and therefore fails to introduce her contribution. The choice mentioning Sayf al-Dawla''s patronage provides a biographical detail but does not convey the broader significance of her astronomical work.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 60, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-19"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-19","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);



Text 2: Environmental sociologist Tomás Herrera cautions that urban greening initiatives, including community gardens, can accelerate gentrification in low-income neighborhoods. As property values rise near newly landscaped areas, longtime residents may face displacement through increased rents, ultimately losing access to the very amenities that were designed to benefit them.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Text 1 emphasizes health benefits measured at the neighborhood level, while Text 2 focuses on socioeconomic consequences that may undermine those benefits for the intended recipients."},{"key":"B","text":"Text 1 presents community gardens as a solution to food insecurity, while Text 2 argues that gardens are ineffective at increasing fresh produce availability."},{"key":"C","text":"Text 1 relies on anecdotal evidence from individual gardeners, while Text 2 draws on large-scale demographic data."},{"key":"D","text":"Text 1 advocates for government funding of green spaces, while Text 2 argues that private investment produces better environmental outcomes."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'Text 1 presents quantitative evidence that community gardens reduce stress-related medical visits, characterizing green spaces as health assets measured across neighborhoods. Text 2 characterizes the same spaces as potential drivers of gentrification that displace the low-income residents they aim to serve. This captures a genuine difference: one text highlights a direct benefit while the other identifies an indirect harm that can negate that benefit. The claim about food insecurity and fresh produce is wrong because neither text mentions food supply or produce availability. The claim that Text 1 uses anecdotal evidence while Text 2 uses demographic data reverses the actual methodological contrast: Text 1 cites a longitudinal study with specific percentages, while Text 2 describes a general socioeconomic mechanism without citing particular data. The claim about government versus private funding is wrong because neither text discusses funding sources or investment models.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"evidence_mismatch"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 80, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-19"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-19","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);




